{
  "title": "Debugging techniques that outlast any framework",
  "content": "After enough code that touches debugging techniques gets reviewed, the same failure pattern keeps showing up. This is the version of the write-up that includes the part that broke. The dashboards look healthy right up until the incident starts.\n\nEvery few years the stack turns over. The language changes, the framework changes, the deployment target changes, and the debugging habits that were load-bearing in the old stack quietly stop working. The teams that recover fastest are not the ones who learned the new framework's error messages earliest. They are the ones who kept a set of techniques that do not depend on which framework is in fashion — because those techniques are about **how systems fail**, not about how a particular library is configured.\n\nThis post is about that durable layer. It assumes you already know how to read a stack trace and add a breakpoint. The real problem it solves is the one that shows up when a bug is real, reproducible only sometimes, and the framework's own logs are telling you nothing useful.\n\n## The one-paragraph version\n\nDebugging is the process of reducing the space of possible explanations until only one survives. Frameworks change which explanations are *likely*; they do not change the underlying categories — state, time, concurrency, boundaries, and data shape. If you learn to test each category deliberately instead of guessing, you will be effective in any language. The techniques below are ordered by how often they pay off, not by how clever they are.\n\n## Why this concept confuses people\n\nMost debugging advice is actually framework advice wearing a general-sounding hat. \"Check your middleware order.\" \"Make sure your effect dependencies are right.\" \"Verify the ORM isn't doing an N+1.\" These are useful, but they expire. When the framework changes, the advice becomes noise, and the developer who memorized it is back to square one.\n\nThe confusion is that people conflate two different skills:\n\n1. **Knowing where bugs hide in a specific tool.** This is fast to learn and fast to lose.\n2. **Knowing how to systematically narrow a hypothesis.** This is slow to learn and never expires.\n\nA concrete example: a developer who has only ever debugged in a single-page app framework may reach for the browser devtools network tab as their first move. Move them to a backend service and they freeze, because their one reliable instrument is gone. The instrument was never the skill. The skill was \"observe the actual bytes crossing the boundary.\"\n\n## The mental model that makes it click\n\nThink of your system as a pipeline of transformations. A request enters, gets parsed, gets routed, gets authorized, touches state, gets serialized, and leaves. A bug is a place where the value you *expect* to flow through diverges from the value that *actually* flows through.\n\nThat gives you one reliable move: **find the last point where the value was correct and the first point where it was wrong.** The bug lives between them. This is binary search applied to data flow, and it is language-agnostic in the same way that sorting is language-agnostic.\n\nEverything else is a way of making that divergence observable. Logging makes it observable after the fact. A debugger makes it observable in the moment. A tracer makes it observable across process boundaries. A minimal reproduction makes it observable without the rest of the system getting in the way.\n\nHere is the decision framework I would hand to someone joining a team, ordered by cost:\n\n1. **Can I reproduce it deterministically?** If not, that is the bug to fix first. Non-deterministic bugs are almost always time, concurrency, or uninitialized state.\n2. **Can I make the failing input smaller?** Strip the request down until it is the minimum that still fails.\n3. **Can I observe the value at the boundary?** Log or breakpoint the input and output of the function you suspect.\n4. **Can I bisect in time?** If it used to work, find the commit where it stopped.\n5. **Can I bisect in space?** If it fails on one machine and not another, diff the environments.\n\nYou will notice none of these mention a framework. That is the point.\n\n## A concrete worked example\n\nAssume a service that returns a user's account balance. A bug report says the balance is sometimes wrong by exactly the amount of a recent transfer. Intermittent, off by a specific amount, no crash.\n\n**Step 1: classify the failure.** Intermittent plus a specific numeric discrepancy points at either a race condition or a caching layer serving stale data. Those are different categories, so test them separately.\n\n**Step 2: test the cache hypothesis.** Read the value directly from the source of truth, bypassing every cache. If the source is correct and the response is wrong, the cache is the suspect. If both are wrong, the write path is the suspect.\n\n**Step 3: test the race hypothesis.** If reads and writes can interleave, look for a read that happens between the debit and the credit of a transfer. This is the classic non-atomic update: two writes that should be one transaction.\n\nHere is what the fix looks like in the abstract, and why it is not framework-specific:\n\n```python\n# WRONG: two separate operations, a reader can observe the gap\nbalance = db.get_balance(user_id)\ndb.set_balance(user_id, balance - amount)\n\n# RIGHT: one atomic operation, no observable intermediate state\ndb.execute(\n    \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - :amount WHERE id = :id\",\n    {\"amount\": amount, \"id\": user_id},\n)\n```\n\nThe first version has a window between the read and the write. Under load, another request reads the old value and writes back a value that ignores the first update. The second version pushes the arithmetic into the database, where it is atomic. The technique — \"look for read-modify-write sequences and check whether the intermediate state is observable\" — is the same in every language with a database behind it. The syntax is not the lesson.\n\nThis is also where security quietly lives. A read-modify-write on a balance is a correctness bug; the same pattern applied to an authorization check is a vulnerability. If a request reads a user's role, then a separate request changes it, the window is a privilege-escalation path. The debugging instinct (\"find the observable intermediate state\") and the security instinct (\"find the time-of-check-to-time-of-use gap\") are the same instinct. Treating them as separate disciplines is how teams end up fixing the same class of bug twice.\n\n## How this connects to things you already know\n\nIf you have ever used `git bisect`, you already know the core technique. You are doing binary search over history to find the commit that introduced a behavior change. The general form is: **when the search space is ordered, halve it.**\n\nIf you have ever added a print statement and then deleted it, you already know instrumentation. The general form is: **make the invisible visible at the point where you need it.**\n\nIf you have ever written a unit test that failed, you already know minimal reproduction. The general form is: **remove everything that is not necessary for the failure.**\n\nThe reason these transfer across stacks is that they are not about the stack. They are about search, observation, and reduction. Frameworks give you new places to apply them; they do not replace them.\n\n## Common misconceptions, corrected\n\n**\"The framework's logging is enough.\"** Framework logs are optimized for the framework's own diagnostics, not for your data flow. They will tell you a request was rejected; they will rarely tell you *which field* was the wrong shape. Add your own boundary logs.\n\n**\"If I can't reproduce it, I can't debug it.\"** You can debug it by making it reproducible. Non-determinism usually comes from a small set of sources: time, concurrency, external state, or uninitialized memory. Each has a standard technique — freeze the clock, serialize the access, stub the dependency, initialize explicitly.\n\n**\"More logs are better.\"** More logs are more noise. The useful unit is a log at a boundary with the value that crossed it. A hundred logs inside a function are worth less than one log at its input and one at its output.\n\n**\"It's probably a framework bug.\"** Occasionally true, almost never the first thing to check. The base rate is that your code is wrong. Verify the framework's behavior against its documentation before assuming it is broken.\n\n**\"Security is a separate review.\"** The same failures that cause incorrect behavior cause vulnerabilities. An unvalidated input that corrupts a calculation is the same unvalidated input that enables injection. Debugging and threat modeling overlap more than most teams admit.\n\n## The advanced version (once the basics are solid)\n\nOnce the fundamentals are automatic, the leverage comes from changing *what you can observe* rather than *what you guess*.\n\n**Distributed tracing.** When a request crosses process boundaries, a stack trace stops at the edge. A trace with a correlation ID follows the request across services. The technique is old; the tooling changes. The durable part is propagating a single identifier through every hop so you can reconstruct the path.\n\n**Deterministic replay.** If you can record the inputs to a system and replay them, you can debug a production failure offline. This is the idea behind record-and-replay debuggers and behind request-capture proxies. The durable part is the idea, not the tool.\n\n**Fault injection.** Instead of waiting for a dependency to fail, make it fail on purpose — add latency, return errors, drop packets. This converts \"we think it handles failure\" into a tested claim. It is the cheapest way to find the bugs that only appear under conditions you cannot reproduce by hand.\n\n**Differential debugging.** Run the same input through two versions of the system and diff the outputs. This is how you find regressions that pass all your tests but change behavior in a way users notice.\n\n**Reading the data, not the code.** When code looks correct and behavior is wrong, inspect the actual stored state. Type mismatches, encoding issues, and stale caches are invisible in source and obvious in data. A surprising number of \"impossible\" bugs are a field that is a string in one place and a number in another.\n\n## Quick reference\n\n| Situation | First move | Durable technique |\n|---|---|---|\n| Works locally, fails in prod | Diff environments and config | Bisect in space |\n| Intermittent, no pattern | Look for time, concurrency, external state | Make it deterministic |\n| Wrong value, no crash | Log value at boundaries | Find last-correct / first-wrong point |\n| Used to work, now doesn't | Find the change | Bisect in time (`git bisect`) |\n| Crosses services | Follow the request | Correlation ID / tracing |\n| Only fails under load | Reproduce the load | Fault injection / concurrency testing |\n| Data looks wrong in the UI | Query the source of truth | Inspect stored state, not code |\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How do I debug a bug I can't reproduce?**\n\nStart by identifying which of four sources is causing the non-determinism: time, concurrency, external state, or uninitialized values. Freeze the clock, serialize the concurrent access, stub the external dependency, or initialize explicitly — one at a time — until the bug becomes reproducible. A bug you can reproduce reliably is a bug you can fix.\n\n**Why does my code work locally but fail in production?**\n\nThe usual causes are environment differences: configuration values, dependency versions, network latency, and data volume. Diff the two environments systematically, starting with configuration and versions. The technique is bisection in space — find the smallest difference that flips the behavior.\n\n**What is the fastest way to find where a value goes wrong?**\n\nFind the last point in the pipeline where the value is correct and the first point where it is wrong. Log or breakpoint the input and output of the function in between. This narrows the search to a single transformation instead of the whole system.\n\n**When should I stop debugging and rewrite?**\n\nWhen you can no longer form a testable hypothesis about the cause, or when the cost of understanding the existing code exceeds the cost of replacing it. Until then, keep narrowing. Rewriting is a last resort, not a first instinct, because you lose the accumulated knowledge embedded in the current code.\n\n## Further reading worth your time\n\n- *Debugging: The 9 Indispensable Rules* by David Agans — short, language-agnostic, still correct.\n- The `git bisect` documentation — the canonical example of binary search applied to history.\n- Any introduction to distributed tracing concepts — the durable part is the correlation ID, not the vendor.\n\n## What to do in the next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your current project and find one function that reads and writes shared state in two separate steps. Add a log line between the read and the write that prints the value and a timestamp. Run your test suite or a load test against it. If you see two reads of the same value before either write completes, you have found the class of bug that no framework upgrade will fix for you.\n\nDebugging outlasts frameworks because it is not about frameworks. It is about search, observation, and reduction — applied with whatever tools the current stack provides. Learn the categories, and every new framework becomes a new place to apply skills you already have.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
